# Consumer-owned schema extension (issue 08 — Task assignment & labels). The
# Assignees / Watchers junctions are M2M link tables whose GraphQL create
# mutations are masked off (sqlgen.yml), so the raw generated mutations can never
# name the two rows to link — exactly the gap addTaskDependency fills for
# task_dependencies. Both junctions also carry a timestamp column beyond their two
# FKs, which makes them ineligible for a nested Task.assignees / Task.watchers
# `connect` (PRD §9.9 E5), so they stay hand-written.
#
# These blessed mutations name both sides and guard tenant isolation themselves:
# the junctions carry no workspace_id, so each resolver resolves the Task through
# the tenant-scoped client first, so a link that spans Workspaces is refused
# before any row is written. Removing a link uses the generated
# deleteTaskAssignee / deleteTaskWatcher, which already name the composite key as
# arguments.
#
# Applying a Label (story 27) is the generated nested mutation
# updateTaskWithRelated(id: $taskID, input: { labels: { connect: [$labelID] } }):
# task_labels is a pure two-FK junction, so it is nestable, and `connect` reads
# the Label tenant-scoped before linking. `labels: { disconnect: [...] }` (or the
# generated deleteTaskLabel) removes one. Resolver bodies live in
# task_relationships.resolvers.go and are preserved across `sqlgen generate` by
# gqlgen's copy-through semantics.

extend type Mutation {
  """
  Assign a User to a Task within the Active Workspace (PRD story 25). The Task
  must be visible in the Active Workspace; the assignment is idempotent-friendly
  via the deleteTaskAssignee counterpart. Returns the created assignment.
  """
  assignUserToTask(taskID: UUID!, userID: UUID!): TaskAssignee!

  """
  Watch a Task to follow its updates without being an Assignee (PRD story 26).
  The watcher is the authenticated caller — never client-supplied — so a member
  watches on their own behalf. The Task must be visible in the Active Workspace.
  """
  watchTask(taskID: UUID!): TaskWatcher!
}
